WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.310
Joe sighed to himself quietly, looking out
of the window at the still, silent stretch

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.290
of highway snaking through the desert only
to disappear somewhere over the horizon.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:15.650
The sun was slowly crawling down, almost completely
vanished from sight, and he knew when it was

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.670
gone, there’d be no light left in the desert.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.119
It wasn’t the same kind of darkness you
found in a city, there they had streetlights

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:26.620
and cars whizzing through with their headlights
on full blast.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:33.210
It never really got dark in the cities, but
out here, nothing but true, pitch-black darkness

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:38.540
for miles around, only alleviated when the
sky was clear enough for the stars to shine

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.809
through, or broken by the beams of a passing
car speeding down the highway.

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:48.550
Giving another sigh, Joe sipped on his coffee;
by this point, it was lukewarm to match the

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.640
dropping temperature outside and bitter to
the taste.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.130
But he needed the caffeine to keep himself
awake.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.890
After all, he had a long night ahead of him,
but then again, they were all long nights

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.510
in this line of work.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:06.190
This was the only diner for miles around,
and he had no idea just how many hours it

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:10.459
would be before he next got to eat or drink
anything.

00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:15.270
With an almost empty pot with the last dregs
of coffee in one hand, the waitress came up

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.819
to Joe at his table.

00:01:16.819 --> 00:01:19.869
It was the last spot in the diner with anyone
still sitting in it.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:22.880
“Closing time,” she told him with a tired
smile.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:23.880
“One for the road?”

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.939
Nodding, Joe lifted his travel mug for her
to fill.

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:29.859
As her workday was ending, his was about to
start.

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:34.529
He grabbed his jacket and slung it over his
shoulder, leaving a few dollars as a tip on

00:01:34.529 --> 00:01:38.999
the table before he made his way out of the
diner and over to his car.

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:43.369
Buckling into the driver’s seat, Joe took
a deep breath as he pulled his phone out of

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:47.390
his pocket, dreading the potential barrage
of notifications that would come flooding

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.649
through the second he did.

00:01:49.649 --> 00:01:54.909
Opening up the corresponding app, he clocked
in for the night, and sure enough, was instantly

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:58.310
met with a veritable tidal wave of requests.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.679
Grumbling quietly to himself, he tapped ‘Accept’
on the first one he saw, and turned the key

00:02:03.679 --> 00:02:05.079
in the ignition.

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:09.989
Working as a driver for a carpool and ride-sharing
service was far from a simple job at the best

00:02:09.989 --> 00:02:15.879
of times, but even more so in this particular
area of the Southwestern United States.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:20.730
The state of New Mexico shared a border not
only with Texas but with Chihuahua, one of

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.269
the Northmost states of Mexico.

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:27.579
Being so close to that border meant that most
of the calls and requests for lifts that Joe

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:32.810
received on an average night meant he needed
to bounce back and forth between the US and

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:37.769
Mexico, crossing the border at his clients’
request – legally, of course.

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:40.709
We’re not going all Breaking Bad here today.

00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:45.569
Some nights weren’t so complicated; someone
might just need a quick ride from Doña Ana

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:51.500
to the town of Truth or Consequences - a New
Mexico town named after, we kid you not, a

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.900
TV game show.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:58.401
Sure, that might have been a seventy-five-mile
drive up the I-25, but at least it didn’t

00:02:58.401 --> 00:03:02.170
involve crossing from El Paso into the city
of Juarez.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:05.579
At this time of night, it was nowhere near
as busy as during the day.

00:03:05.579 --> 00:03:10.300
But even so, that didn’t make hopping back
and forth over the border, and all the red

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.931
tape it entailed, any less of an arduous process,
especially stuck behind the wheel of a car

00:03:15.931 --> 00:03:17.079
for hours on end.

00:03:17.079 --> 00:03:19.730
Tonight, however, wasn’t a simple night.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:24.239
Joe’s first call was for a pickup from a
couple whose flight had been rerouted to the

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:25.889
international airport in Juarez.

00:03:25.889 --> 00:03:31.079
They seemed to be newlyweds and demanded to
be taken all the way back to a hotel in Las

00:03:31.079 --> 00:03:36.109
Cruces, making it abundantly clear they didn’t
want to stay closer to the airport and outright

00:03:36.109 --> 00:03:40.130
refusing when Joe offered to recommend them
some nearby hotels.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:44.260
Getting to know most of the area around the
three-way intersection between New Mexico,

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.680
Texas, and the Mexican Border after driving
around it so many times, he’d gotten to

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.770
know the area pretty well.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:55.579
Unfortunately, not everyone wanted to hear
a display of Joe’s local knowledge; most

00:03:55.579 --> 00:03:58.719
of them just wanted him to drive them in silence.

00:03:58.719 --> 00:04:03.629
Next up, a group of rowdy guys, real frat
house types from the University of El Paso,

00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:07.150
Texas, wanted to be taken to a bar halfway
across the city.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:11.340
A bit more of a straightforward trip, Joe
thought, although he could have done without

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.830
them drunkenly chanting ‘Spring Break’
at the top of their lungs every five minutes.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:19.400
After that, it was another jaunt across the
border, this time transporting a suit and

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.610
Stetson-wearing Texan gentleman to Horizon
City.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:27.220
He spent the whole ride on his phone, talking
at length about a business deal he’d just

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.430
closed, not saying a word to Joe the entire
time.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.080
Doug Dimmadome, eat your heart out.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.470
The next person to request a lift wasn’t
much more talkative either, she was a twenty-something

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:42.699
girl who plainly stated she just wanted to
get far away from Sierra Blanca.

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:46.810
Hours and hours had ticked by since Joe had
left the diner, the last of the coffee in

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.759
his travel mug having been finished long before.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:54.169
His next ride request had come through and
required him to once again make the drive

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:55.730
out to the other side of the border.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:05:00.539
By this point, the evening had turned into
night and was now approaching the earliest

00:05:00.539 --> 00:05:06.270
hours of the following morning, the desert
still filled with nothing but deep, inky blackness.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:12.139
The same desert that had scorched acres upon
acres of skin, that had taken so many lives,

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:16.629
big and small, and picked the skin and flesh
from countless bones.

00:05:16.629 --> 00:05:21.490
The desert can be a truly merciless place
at the best of times, but sometimes, there’s

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:25.340
a little something more out there, just waiting
for you.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:26.830
Dark and hungry.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:31.600
Occasionally, as he drove back towards El
Paso, Joe would see another car speeding towards

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.290
him on the other side of the highway, but
it was so dark that he could see nothing of

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:36.440
the driver.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.270
Only their headlights were visible, right
up until they drove past him, everything behind

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:45.720
the hood of the oncoming car almost imperceptible
as it whizzed through the night.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.970
Crossing the border so many times a night,
Joe had become friendly with a few of the

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:53.960
officers that patrolled the gates, waving
cars past after they’d shown the necessary

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:54.960
paperwork.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.570
He was never usually held there for long enough
to start actual conversations with them, exchanging

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:04.500
only a friendly nod or one-liner with the
few Border Patrol guards whose names he could

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:05.810
still remember.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:10.310
But sometimes, when things were a little quieter
at the gates, he’d overhear the odd passing

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:13.250
comment while his driver side window was rolled
down.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.919
Tonight, as the long barrier in front of his
car was raised, Joe caught a few words coming

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:22.370
through on a radio belonging to one of the
guards, something vague about sightings of

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:23.370
‘phantom lights’.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:28.510
He chuckled to himself, writing it off as
yet another alleged alien sighting.

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:32.139
Something about the desert at night really
seemed to capture people’s imaginations,

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:36.830
Joe thought to himself as he pulled away from
the border gate, heading for the I-10.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:41.419
There were always stories like that circulating
around this area of New Mexico.

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:52.270
It was easy to see why, being so close to
Roswell, the home of Area 51; a United States

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:57.460
Air Force base that secretly housed alien
life forms and spaceships, allegedly at least,

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.600
if years of conspiracy believers and Facebook
memers are to be believed.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.819
Every so often one of Joe’s passengers would
ask him about Unidentified Flying Objects

00:07:06.819 --> 00:07:11.060
or he’d overhear them talking amongst themselves
about a slew of urban legends.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:15.520
Phantom hitchhikers that would hitch rides
on the highway, only to vanish from the backseats

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:16.520
of cars.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:22.430
And, of course, tales of disembodied floating
lights, either hovering way up in the sky

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:24.250
or just above the ground.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:28.630
Regardless of whichever one was popular at
any given moment, Joe knew there were plenty

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.860
of weird and spooky stories coming out of
the desert.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:36.229
What he was unaware of, however, was that
he was currently in one of his own.

00:07:36.229 --> 00:07:40.240
He'd been so wrapped up in thoughts of what
he might do if one of the people that hired

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.409
him turned out to be a wandering spirit that
Joe didn’t even notice he had made a wrong

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:45.409
turn.

00:07:45.409 --> 00:07:49.620
It was only by the virtue of glancing at his
car’s GPS that he even realized, and by

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:54.669
then it had been long enough since that he
was completely unsure how far back it was

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:56.449
that he had made such an error.

00:07:56.449 --> 00:07:58.770
According to the map, he was… nowhere.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:03.810
He hadn’t stopped, his car was still moving
at a steady speed down the long stretch of

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.419
asphalt, but the GPS didn’t show any road
at his current location.

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:12.000
He had no clue which part of which highway
he was even on.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.440
Short on options, and short on time to get
to his next client for the night, Joe just

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.909
did what his job required of him: he kept
on driving.

00:08:19.909 --> 00:08:23.840
Anyone watching from a distance would have
seen an empty, lengthy portion of the highway,

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:28.540
with but one single car traveling along it,
only the headlights both illuminating the

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.960
road in front of it… and giving away the
car’s position.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:36.360
Slowing down slightly, just to play it safe,
Joe tried searching up where he was using

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.030
the maps app on his phone.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:42.580
He figured he wouldn’t get stopped for checking
his phone while driving, or be much risk to

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:46.900
any other drivers on the road; after all,
it seemed like his was the only car around

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.320
for miles.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.610
Even if he was still heading in near enough
the right direction for his next pickup, that

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:54.970
would be at least some saving grace.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:59.320
Maybe the map on the car’s built-in GPS
was a little outdated, he thought, and that

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:02.220
was preventing this part of the highway from
showing up.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:06.470
Connection to the internet was extremely limited
this far out in the New Mexico desert, and

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:11.030
by the time the map loaded, it didn’t provide
much in the way of new information or even

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.890
the slightest bit of comforting reassurance.

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:19.650
It only seemed to agree with the GPS that
Joe was well and truly lost.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:23.510
As he took his free hand off the wheel for
a split second, just to try and zoom in on

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:27.970
the satellite view of the terrain, two bright,
white lights appeared in the distance.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:32.270
Startled, Joe dropped his phone into the front
passenger seat, hearing it slip onto the floor

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:37.190
of the car as he gripped the steering wheel
again, both hands back at ten and two.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:41.600
The lights were speeding in the opposite direction
he was driving in, a fair few feet ahead of

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.580
him, but still, it was another vehicle on
this otherwise empty, isolated stretch of

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:47.580
the highway.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:52.320
A loud and sudden idea rang out in Joe’s
head; if he could signal what he had assumed

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.110
to be another approaching car, he could ask
the driver where exactly he was, and what

00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:59.870
the quickest route to get back to the I-10
was.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:03.880
He couldn’t guarantee he would make the
oncoming vehicle stop and help him, after

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:07.460
all, so few people would willingly trust a
stranger on the highway.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:11.540
But considering his only other options were
to either keep driving along this seemingly

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:16.300
endless road, or to turn back around and try
to find wherever it was he’d made the wrong

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:21.150
turning in the first place, needless to say
Joe’s hands were a little tied.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:24.710
Tapping the appropriate switch affixed to
his wheel, he flickered his own headlights

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:29.100
on and off, in an effort to catch the other
driver’s attention.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:33.570
Nothing, the pair of headlights kept their
current course, both theirs and Joe’s traveling

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:37.770
parallel to each other as if they were about
to joust with their beams of light.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.220
Hitting the switch again, Joe tried lowering
his headlight’s setting and then raising

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.170
it back to full brightness.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:44.900
Still no reaction from the other driver.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:49.020
He pushed the emergency button, that activated
both the front and rear lights of the car,

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.450
but this only yielded the same result.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:55.640
Deciding there was nothing else that he could
do, Joe gently pulled his vehicle over, parking

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.740
abruptly at the side of the highway.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:02.730
He instinctively checked his rearview, only
to remember there wasn’t anyone behind,

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.980
just him and the apparently oblivious driver
approaching ahead.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:10.460
In a last-ditch effort to get their attention,
Joe punched the car horn, rolling down his

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:12.880
window in order to listen for a response.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:17.120
The blaring noise echoed through the empty
desert night, a loud and sustained blast followed

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.540
by a beat of silence as Joe paused, waiting
to hear the same sound back from the oncoming

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:23.540
vehicle.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.200
He squinted at it through the dark.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.460
It was still pretty far, but closing rapidly.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:32.461
Or, as far as he could tell it was, all he
had to go on was the advancing lights, with

00:11:32.461 --> 00:11:34.410
nothing visible behind them.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:38.670
Joe leaned out of his window, hoping that
would somehow give him better visibility through

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:40.660
the sheer darkness of a desert at night.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:45.750
It did as little to improve things as a second,
much longer blast of his horn did.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.610
The other driver still didn’t react.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:51.850
By now, the lights were close enough that
whoever was behind the other wheel would be

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:53.170
able to see Joe.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:57.800
And yet, they didn’t do anything to change
their speed, didn’t flicker their lights

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.120
back or sound their own horn.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:04.880
Nothing, just the same two points of light;
they might as well have been floating just

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.350
above the ground given how they obscured what
was behind them.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:12.370
At this point, Joe was certain they were close
enough to see him now, little more than a

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:17.100
few feet in front of his stationary car and
still closing at a sustained speed.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:21.290
Getting agitated, he went to hit his horn
a third time, when he noticed something…

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:25.000
or rather, he noticed something that he hadn’t.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:29.000
This car, hurtling towards him through the
night, wasn’t making any sound.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:32.640
It wasn’t just that the other driver deliberately
hadn’t returned the signals Joe had been

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.710
trying to give; there was no noise coming
from it whatsoever.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:40.380
No grumbling of an engine, nor the rolling
of rubber tires against the asphalt surface

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:41.510
of the highway.

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:46.240
It was silent, just the pair of ominous lights
soundlessly racing through the night…

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:51.960
heading straight for where Joe was sat, parked
still; a sitting duck, like he was defenseless

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:52.960
prey.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:56.670
Violently turning the key in the ignition,
Joe slammed his foot down on the accelerator.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:59.100
His car spluttered to life and careened down
the road.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.410
He was heading straight for the other car
– if it even was a car – hoping to shoot

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:07.400
straight past it and take his chances continuing
along the highway alone.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:11.500
The lights were almost on top of him now,
still too bright to make out anything behind

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:12.500
them.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:17.121
But there was still no engine noise, nothing
to indicate what the hell it even was… until

00:13:17.121 --> 00:13:20.010
one of them jumped onto the hood of his car.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:24.110
An orb of light, attached to a thrashing shape
beneath, parted from the other.

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:28.620
It leaped through the air and came down right
in the path of Joe’s car, just as he was

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.730
about to pass what he’d first thought to
be another car.

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:35.730
The light smashed directly through the windshield,
Joe instinctively lifting an arm to cover

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:39.220
his face and protect his eyes from the impending
shower of broken glass.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:43.470
But it was in that second that he took his
eyes off the road that the other light made

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:44.470
its move.

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:49.830
It had been perfectly parallel with Joe’s
car, only to turn, sharply and violently,

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:53.620
connecting with the driver’s side door with
enough force to not only dent the metal surface

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:55.740
but to completely lift the car up.

00:13:55.740 --> 00:14:00.810
It barrelled sideways, tumbling over itself
off of the highway and into the surrounding

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:01.810
desert.

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:06.230
Trapped inside, bucked tight, Joe was thrown
about in his seat, unable to steady himself

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:07.590
as the car rolled.

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:11.990
His arm was still over his face, splinters
of glass showering over him.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:17.600
The… whatever it was, was still thrashing
about, its glow illuminating the entire interior

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.010
of the car.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:23.740
Until the whole vehicle came to a stop, landing
upside down, wheels in the air pointing up

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:25.590
at the cloudy night sky.

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:29.940
Hanging the wrong way up, Joe reached for
his seatbelt and unclasped it, his head instantly

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:32.580
dropping onto the ceiling of the upturned
car.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:36.270
Through the shattered driver-side window,
he crawled out on his hands.

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:40.180
The pain was shooting through his whole body,
bruises and blood covering his arms, and the

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:44.400
intense, agonizing feeling of broken bones
on top of that.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.430
Rolling over onto his back, Joe looked up
at the wreck of his car, his livelihood…

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:50.910
and the monster standing in front of it.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:54.540
Illuminated fully for the first time by the
car’s still active headlights, the thing

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.390
had managed to pull itself free from the destroyed
windshield and was now pacing slowly around

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:00.850
the overturned hood.

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:05.830
It walked on two legs but didn’t move like
a man, in fact, it didn’t even look like

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:06.830
one.

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:10.830
It was like something pulled straight out
of prehistoric times, a pair of hinged limbs

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:15.540
supporting a streamlined reptilian body, with
hooked claws on its feet.

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:20.830
Its back was arched, keeping itself low, as
its two arms stayed close to its center.

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:25.490
The creature had almost human-like hands,
but each finger was tipped with long, pointed

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:26.560
talons.

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:31.070
But its head was what Joe saw first, what
he had seen at a distance and mistaken for

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:33.650
car headlights only a few minutes ago.

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:38.320
In the place he would’ve expected to see
a pair of slitted eyes staring at him, was

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.720
a bulbous mass of a head.

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:45.270
Bio-luminescent light glowed from beneath
the monster’s skin, a brain visible amongst

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:51.440
a web of bright organs… and beneath that,
a wide, gaping maw of sharp teeth.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:55.930
Joe noticed another light drawing near, brightening
the area where he lay defenseless.

00:15:55.930 --> 00:16:00.160
The second of the raptor-like monsters was
pacing closer to him, both their glowing heads

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:04.820
kept low to the ground, ready to strike.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:07.030
***
“Yep, looks pretty clear to me,” one of

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:09.880
the men disguised as a Highway Patrol officer
announced.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:13.800
“Handprints match up with what their front
paws look like, no trace of the driver.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.390
Just a big wreck in the middle of nowhere.”

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:20.920
“Better get on the horn to control then,”
his colleague replied, wearing a similar uniform.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:24.580
“I thought SCP-745s were only found a little
north of here.”

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:28.670
“We shut off the section of the highway
they usually show up at,” the first Foundation

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:32.910
agent explained casually, sipping his coffee
as he looked at the upturned wreckage of Joe’s

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:35.210
car; his body was nowhere to be found.

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.980
“But every now and then, a couple of them
will show up a bit away from there.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:42.680
One of them wouldn’t come this far into
the south of New Mexico on its own though.”

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:44.360
“Oh, because of the lights, right?”

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:47.820
his fellow agent responded, the two of them
walking back towards their car.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:50.510
“Their heads, they mimic car headlights
or something.”

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:54.220
“Well, there’s that,” the first man
nodded, taking one last look at the long,

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:56.821
open desert around them, and the highway stretching
through it.

00:16:56.821 --> 00:17:00.480
“Plus it must get lonely traveling out here
on your own.”

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:07.100
Now go and check out “SCP-1265 THE MESOZOIC
PRESERVE” and “SCP-682 TERRIFYING TEST

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:12.019
LOGS” to continue on a Jurassic journey
filled with more rampaging reptilian creatures

00:17:12.019 --> 00:17:14.569
and other ravenous raptor-like anomalies!

